Short:            Multi-codec video player, AGA to PiStorm
Uploader:         Darren Banfi <boingball@gmail.com>
Author:           Darren "boingball" Banfi
Type:             gfx/show
Version:          0.1
Requires:         Kickstart 2.0+; MintAMP-compatible audio.library setup for
                  MP3/AAC; AmiSSL v5 + amisslmaster.library for HTTPS/HLS/
                  YouTube (optional, only needed if built with SSL=1)
Architecture:     m68k-amigaos >= 68030

Description:

MintVID is a video player for real 68k Amiga hardware, built in the spirit
of MintAMP (the libhelix-based audio player): a small portable C core with
thin, Amiga-specific display/audio layers around it.

WHO THIS IS FOR

Anyone still running actual Amiga hardware or an FPGA/PiStorm-class
accelerator who wants to play video files, watch YouTube, or browse IPTV
channels directly on the Amiga - not just decode a file format nobody has
anymore. One binary runs everywhere; nothing is locked to a CPU tier - every
codec below will run on any supported machine, it is decode speed that
decides what is actually watchable:

  - Stock A600 / plain AGA, 68030, no FPU, no RTG - Cinepak plays smoothly
    at small frame sizes with AGA C2P/dither or HAM output. Heavier codecs
    (MJPEG, MPEG-4 Part 2) will decode, but crawl - roughly 7 fps on a bare
    68060, let alone a 68030 - not something worth watching.
  - 68040/060 with RTG (CyberGraphX/Picasso96) - Cinepak, MJPEG, MPEG-1/2
    and MPEG-4 Part 2 become watchable at modest sizes. H.264/AVC will still
    decode, just nowhere near fast enough to be worth it without PiStorm.
  - PiStorm/Emu68 (ARM-hosted 68k) with RTG - the same player, but H.264/AVC
    (Baseline through High Profile, CABAC, B-frames) becomes realistic.
    Field-confirmed on a Pi3-based PiStorm 600: anything under roughly 200p
    (e.g. YouTube/IPTV live streams) plays well.

There is no separate "PiStorm build" or "AGA build" to choose between - one
binary, and you pick what to play based on what your machine can actually
keep up with.

WHAT IT PLAYS

  - Containers: AVI, QuickTime MOV/MP4, Matroska/MKV, MPEG-TS/M2TS, raw
    MPEG-1 program streams, raw MJPEG/MPEG-4 Visual streams, and direct
    HTTP(S)/HLS URLs (including live IPTV playlists and YouTube).
  - Video codecs: Cinepak, Microsoft Video 1, Microsoft RLE8, uncompressed
    UYVY422, H.263 baseline, Motion-JPEG, MPEG-1, MPEG-2 (with B-frames),
    MPEG-4 Part 2 (ISO ASP and Microsoft MP42/DIV2), and H.264/AVC Baseline/
    Main/High Profile.
  - Audio to Paula: PCM, MP2, MP3, AAC-LC (raw/ADTS/LATM), and fixed-point
    AC-3 (stereo downmix) - all confirmed good quality on real hardware.
  - Two complete GUI sets over the same player: ReAction (V44) and plain
    GadTools/Intuition (V37) for a stock 3.0 install, each with matching
    file, IPTV-directory and YouTube-search browser windows.

Every codec decoder in this package is validated frame-by-frame against
ffmpeg's own decoder on a Linux host before it ever ships, and the portable
core is additionally cross-built and run under a real big-endian m68k target
(gcc-m68k-linux-gnu + qemu-user) to catch endianness/alignment bugs that a
little-endian x86 host build cannot - see the project page for details.

WHAT IT IS NOT

Not a decoder for one fixed format - it is a plugin architecture, so the
codec list above will grow. Not a video *editor* or *encoder*: use ffmpeg
or similar on a modern machine to prepare source material, then play the
result on the Amiga. Timeline seeking (cursor left/right, 10 seconds at a
time, landing on the nearest keyframe) works for local QuickTime MOV/MP4
files; AVI, MKV, and network/live sources don't have a keyframe index yet,
so cursor-right stays a fast-forward toggle there instead. Cursor up/down
adjust Paula's volume, and once playback starts, the controller's Info:
field - and, on RTG (CGX/P96), the video window's own title bar - mirrors
a live H:MM:SS/M:SS playhead so you can see where a seek actually landed.

INSTALLATION

Copy the whole archive to your Amiga (or a shared PiStorm volume) and
double-click MintVID - that is the whole program, with IPTV and YouTube
browsing reachable from its own menus. Everything else in the drawer
(mrplay, iptvgui, ytgui, the GadTools -GT set, mr_decode) is a support
binary MintVID or the -GT controller loads on demand (a stock OS 3.0
system without ReAction V44 should instead run MintVID-GT, the plain
GadTools/Intuition equivalent). Keep the whole drawer together - nothing
here is meant to be split up or copied out on its own.

SOURCE / LICENSING

MintVID's own source is MIT-licensed and available from its project
repository. It links several dependencies that keep their own licences
(GPL-2.0-or-later, Apache-2.0, and RealNetworks RPSL among them) - see the
project's README for the full breakdown before redistributing binaries.

This player was inspired by RiVA 0.54 (Fellner/Toeroek/Richter), the
fastest 68k MPEG-1 player, previously distributed on Aminet - but is a
fresh, unrelated codebase and does not include or depend on RiVA's source.

SUPPORT

If MintVID is useful, or YouTube on an Amiga made you laugh, you can help
keep the hardware testing and development going at:
